\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf0 This subsection enumerates major decisions that cut across multiple parts of the spec. Each must eventually be resolved and recorded in the Decision Log (16.8). 16.1.1 System & Deployment Architecture (Monolith vs Microservices) Decision: Overall service topology for Enterprise Mode (Local Mode is intrinsically monolithic). Option A \'96 Monolith-first with deliberate seams Consolidated service (or small set of services) hosting:\
Orchestrator, workspaces, domain APIs, driver coordination, Policy Engine, Ledger.\
\uc0\u9675  Pros: simpler deployment, fewer failure modes, faster iteration, easier end-to-end tracing.\
\uc0\u9675  Cons: tighter coupling; harder to scale specific domains independently.\
\'95 Option B \'96 Plane/Domain Microservices\
Separate services per plane/domain (Orchestrator, Policy, Ledger, Drivers, Workspaces, Meta-Stack).\
\'a0\
\'a0\
\'95 Sepa ate se\
ces pe p a e/do a (O c est ato , o cy, edge , e s, o spaces, eta Stac ) Pros: strong isolation and clear fault domains; targeted scaling; per-service technology choices.\
\uc0\u9675  Cons: heavier operational complexity, cross-service coordination, version skew risk.\
\uc0\u9675  Current leaning:\
Adopt Monolith-first with explicit \'93service seams\'94:\
Code organization and APIs must anticipate future service extraction.\
\'95 Initial deployments: one service per role (core API, worker, UI) with clear internal boundaries between planes.\
\'95 These seams and the eventual migration path must be documented in the Decision Log.\
16.1.2 Model & Reasoning Fabric This covers multi-provider strategy, model abstraction, reasoning trace format, and default reasoning depth/cost controls. (a) Single vs Multi-Provider Model Strategy Decision: How aggressively to pursue a multi-LLM stack vs standardizing on a primary provider family (e.g., GPT-5.1). Option A \'96 Single-provider first Fastest implementation; deeper optimization for one stack.\
\uc0\u9675  Risk: vendor lock-in, sensitivity to pricing/policy shifts.\
\'95 Option B \'96 Multi-provider from day one\
Pluggable routing across OpenAI, Anthropic, local models, etc.\
\uc0\u9675  Higher complexity in testing, routing, safety, and cost modeling.\
\'95 Current direction:\
Design the Model Layer as fully pluggable, but operationally start with one primary provider plus obvious local-model hooks. Multi-provider routing is an explicit vNext capability but must not require architectural rewrites. (b) Model Provider Abstraction Depth Decision: How strongly to standardize the Model Provider interface vs allowing provider-specific behavior. Options:\
(A) Strict, uniform \'93LLM-as-RPC\'94 abstraction (minimal feature surface).\
\uc0\u9675  (B) Layered abstraction: common core plus provider-specific capability surfaces (tools, modalities, fine-tuning\
hooks).\
\uc0\u9675  (C) Model-by-model custom integrations with thin shared wrappers.\
\'95 Trade-offs:\
(A) Simplifies reasoning, testing, and policy enforcement but may waste advanced capabilities.\
\uc0\u9675  (B) Harder to implement, but exploits specialized features while preserving a stable core.\
\uc0\u9675  (C) Maximizes short-term flexibility but fractures the ecosystem and complicates governance.\
\'95 Current leaning:\
(B) Layered abstraction:\
Unified interfaces for core operations (chat, embeddings, tools, streaming, cost/latency metadata).\
\'95 Provider-specific capability surfaces behind feature flags and policy controls.\
\'95 (c) TRF Representation Format\
Decision: How TRF (Targeted Reasoning Fabric) traces are represented and stored. Options:\
Custom structured format optimized for OS Dashboard (Capsules, Ledger, Canon).\
\uc0\u9675  Existing formal proof languages / theorem-prover formats.\
\uc0\u9675  Hybrid: internal format with bridges to external tools.\
\'95 Trade-offs:\
Custom format \uc0\u8594  maximum alignment to Canon concepts but requires bespoke tooling.\
\uc0\u9675  Existing formats \u8594  easier integration with proof/verification systems but may mismatch OS semantics.\
\uc0\u9675  Hybrid \u8594  more complexity, but best of both worlds.\
\'95 Status:\
Deliberately open. v1 may use a pragmatic JSON schema; long-term direction is a hybrid with translation adapters to formal tools. (d) Default Reasoning Depth & Cost Controls Decision: How much deep reasoning (multi-step TRF/daemons) is invoked by default. Trade-off: quality vs latency/cost vs user controllability.\
\'95 Current direction:\
Default to shallow-to-moderate reasoning for everyday UX.\
\'95 Explicit \'93Deep Dive / Research Mode\'94 toggles per workspace to authorize deeper multi-step reasoning and cost.\
\'95 Per-tenant and per-workspace budgets enforced via the AI Billing & Usage Fabric\
\'a0\
\'a0\
\'95 Per tenant and per workspace budgets enforced via the AI Billing & Usage Fabric.\
16.1.3 Environment Representation & Runtime Isolation This covers environment specifications, containerization strategy, and sandbox isolation. (a) Environment Representation: Package Specs vs Containers vs Hybrid Decision: Primary representation of environments for Projects, Capsules, and Blueprints. Options:\
(A) Package-manager\'96driven specs (apt/brew/choco, pip/conda, etc.).\
\uc0\u9675  (B) Container images (Docker/OCI) as canonical execution envelopes.\
\uc0\u9675  (C) Hybrid: canonical environment specs that can be realized via package managers or containers.\
\'95 Trade-offs:\
Package-driven:\
Pros: aligns with developer reality, natural for desktop OS; integrates directly with Package & Environment Drivers. \'a7 Cons: platform variance, dependency conflict risk, weaker reproducibility.\
\uc0\u9675  Container-driven:\
Pros: strong isolation, reproducibility, convergence with infra and HPC. \'a7 Cons: heavier UX for Local Mode; container daemons and permissions increase surface area.\
\uc0\u9675  Hybrid:\
Pros: flexible migration path, local-to-cloud symmetry, tenant-level choice. \'a7 Cons: more complexity in tooling, policy, and observability.\
\'95 Current leaning:\
(C) Hybrid, with:\
Environment Blueprint schema as canonical.\
\'95 Local Mode \uc0\u8594  package-first, optional containers for risky workloads.\
\'95 Enterprise Mode \uc0\u8594  container-first for shared infra; package drivers for developer workstations.\
\'95 (b) Standardization of Environment Specs\
Decision: Whether to reuse devcontainer/Nix-like standards vs define a bespoke blueprint optimized for Capsules. Trade-offs:\
Existing standards \uc0\u8594  easy ecosystem integration, tooling reuse.\
\uc0\u9675  Bespoke \u8594  richer semantics (risk levels, cost hints, Capsule metadata) but more to maintain.\
\'95 Status:\
Open; must be resolved early and logged. v1 may adopt a devcontainer-compatible superset if feasible. (c) Sandbox Isolation Level Decision: Default isolation for executing Capsules and high-risk commands. Options:\
(A) Containerization by default (namespaces, cgroups, etc.).\
\uc0\u9675  (B) Lightweight process-level sandboxing (especially in Local Mode).\
\uc0\u9675  (C) Contextual: policy-driven choice per Capsule/driver.\
\'95 Trade-offs:\
Stronger isolation \uc0\u8594  more safety, but resource overhead and UX complexity.\
\'95 Lightweight sandbox \uc0\u8594  easier on developer machines, weaker boundaries.\
\'95 Current direction:\
Contextual, with policy-driven isolation and clear trust tiers:\
Local Mode: lightweight sandbox by default, with optional containers for \'93Governed\'94 Capsules.\
\'95 Enterprise Mode: container-first for anything infra-touching or multi-tenant.\
\'95 16.1.4 Driver & Execution Layer (Granularity & Safety)\
Decision: Granularity and structure of drivers for OS, tools, and infra. Driver granularity One generic shell / system_exec vs distinct drivers:\
shell.git, shell.ffmpeg, shell.systemctl, cloud-provider drivers, robot drivers, etc.\
\uc0\u9675  Trade-off: simpler registry vs more precise policies, pricing, observability, and safety harnesses.\
