\'a0\
\'95 Attach Safety Harnesses per tool.\
Express per-command budgets and approvals. \'a7 Audit and bill at meaningful semantic boundaries. \'a7 Status:\
Open; v1 may start with a small but explicit set of coarse drivers and progressively refine into more granular drivers as policies mature. 16.1.5 Capsule Runtime Semantics & DSL Expressiveness Decision: Execution semantics, determinism, and language expressiveness for Capsules. (a) Runtime Semantics Open aspects:\
Strength of determinism guarantees (same inputs \uc0\u8594  same outputs).\
\uc0\u9675  Allowed side-effects (filesystem, network, OS processes).\
\uc0\u9675  Versioning and compatibility across Capsule and environment upgrades.\
\uc0\u9675  Required test coverage for marketplace vs internal Capsules.\
\'95 Trade-off:\
Stricter semantics \uc0\u8594  better reproducibility and auditability, but higher authoring burden.\
\uc0\u9675  Looser semantics \u8594  faster authoring and flexibility, but weaker guarantees.\
\'95 Current leaning:\
Define tiered Capsule classes:\
\'93Light\'94 Capsules: personal, experimental automations; weaker guarantees; relaxed sandbox constraints.\
\'95 \'93Governed\'94 Capsules: stricter contracts, test requirements, and safety harnesses; required for marketplace, infra, and\
regulated use.\
\'95 Policy Engine decides which Capsule classes are permitted per tenant/context.\
(b) Capsule DSL Expressiveness Decision: How powerful the Capsule DSL is. Options:\
Minimal DAG-style DSL:\
Steps, dependencies, retries, simple conditionals. \'a7 Loops or complex branching handled by orchestrator logic or code capsules.\
\uc0\u9675  Rich DSL:\
Loops, branching, exceptions, event hooks, and advanced control flow inside the Capsule definition.\
\'95 Trade-off:\
Rich DSL risks becoming a general-purpose programming language with all attendant complexity.\
\uc0\u9675  Minimal DSL forces complex logic into code, but keeps Capsules inspectable and governable.\
\'95 Current leaning:\
v1: DAG + guarded conditionals + retries + simple branching; no arbitrary loops.\
\'95 Revisit loops and more advanced constructs in vNext with strong patterns (e.g., map/reduce templates).\
\'95 16.1.6 Data, Knowledge, Ledger & Truth Representation\
This covers the project/capsule/research graphs, Ledger implementation, retention, and truth modeling. (a) Graph Representation Decision: Whether to store \'93project graph + Capsule graph + research graph + citation graph\'94 as:\
A single unified graph store with typed subgraphs, or\
\'95 Multiple logical graphs over a shared index/storage substrate.\
