\cf2 19.2.3 Safety Harness Patterns for High-Risk Commands & Drivers\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\cf0 A Safety Harness is a policy-bound constraint bundle attached to a driver (and optionally Capsule/tenant) that restricts what operations/arguments/paths are allowed. Generic Safety Harness Template harness_id: harness.unix.high_risk_fs target_driver: "driver.exec.unix" rules:\
- name: "block_rm_root"\
match:\
command: "rm" args_pattern: ["-rf", "/"] action: "deny"\
- name: "restrict_rm"\
match:\
command: "rm" constraints:\
allowed_paths:\
- "/tmp/osdash/**"\
- "/home/*/tmp/**"\
action: "require_approval" required_roles:\
- "SecurityEngineer"\
- name: "log_all"\
match:\
any: true action: "log_only" severity: "high" Example \'96 Package Manager Harness harness_id: harness.pkg.kernel_guard target_driver: "driver.pm.*" rules:\
- name: "block_kernel_install"\
match:\
operation: "package.install" package_pattern: "kernel*" action: "deny"\
- name: "require_approval_for_upgrade"\
match:\
operation: "package.upgrade" action: "require_approval" required_roles:\
- "PlatformEngineer"\
- "CISO"\
Safety Harnesses are first-class objects referenced from:\
Capsule manifests (safety_harness).\
\'95 Policy Packs (e.g., \'93critical infra harness pack\'94).\
\'95 Conformance tests (19.9.3).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

